    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s imperial age, founded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> c. 1550 BCE, stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>.</li><li>Nubian gold, Levantine tribute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>' great-power diplomacy made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> world.</li><li>It ended not by conquest but by dissolution, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Bronze Age Collapse</a> and the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> priesthood hollowed out royal power.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> made peace in 1258 BCE in the earliest treaty to survive in both parties' versions, settling a stalemate after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

