    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmose-i.jpg" alt="Ahmose I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_Ahmose_I_MET_DP140854.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Kingdom emerged from the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, an Asiatic dynasty that had controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> for over a century during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, completed this expulsion around 1550 BCE, reunited the country, reconquered Nubian territories lost during the preceding fragmentation, and began rebuilding the administrative and military institutions that would sustain the empire for nearly five centuries. His successors pressed aggressively outward: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, the first pharaoh to do so, and carried Egyptian arms beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">fourth Nile cataract</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, who ruled as pharaoh alongside the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> from approximately 1479 to 1458 BCE, consolidated this inheritance through trade rather than war, dispatching the celebrated expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and sponsoring an extraordinary building programme at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>. Her death in 1458 BCE was followed by a rebellion of Canaanite and Syrian rulers led by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a>, who evidently doubted the resolve of her successor. Thutmose III would prove the most consequential military pharaoh in Egyptian history, and the empire he built provided the territorial foundation upon which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>'s golden age rested.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">René Hourdry, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Kingdom economy rested on three interlocking foundations: Nubian gold, Levantine tribute, and agricultural surplus from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> floodplain. Nubian gold in particular underwrote the entire imperial superstructure, funding monumental construction, sustaining diplomatic gift exchanges with Near Eastern powers, and maintaining the army. The temple complexes, most prominently <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, functioned simultaneously as religious centres, economic institutions, and state treasuries, accumulating land, livestock, and labour obligations on a scale that eventually made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> priesthood a rival power to the crown. By the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harris-papyrus">Great Harris Papyrus</a>, the temples of Amun held roughly two-thirds of all temple lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and some ninety percent of temple-owned ships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punt-expedition">Punt expedition</a> exemplified the state's direct involvement in long-distance trade, bringing back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> trees, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and exotic animals. The policy begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> of raising the sons of conquered rulers as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at the pharaonic court served both diplomatic and economic ends, producing a class of Egyptianised client princes who governed peripheral territories at minimal cost to the crown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="Battle of Kadesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Abou_Simbel6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> remains the defining military figure of the New Kingdom, conducting some seventeen campaigns over twenty years, beginning with his decisive victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Battle of Megiddo</a> in 1457 BCE. His march through the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aruna-pass">Aruna pass</a> to surprise a coalition of Canaanite rulers led by the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> demonstrated a willingness to accept high operational risk for decisive strategic effect. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, introduced into Egyptian warfare during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> period and subsequently developed into a sophisticated combined-arms instrument, formed the operational backbone of New Kingdom expansion. The largest single engagement of the era was the Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BCE, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> led an estimated 20,000 soldiers against the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> and his thousands of chariots. The battle was tactically indecisive, and the peace treaty of 1258 BCE, concluded with Muwatalli's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and the earliest such treaty to survive in both parties' versions, effectively acknowledged strategic stalemate. The empire's final military test came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, who repelled land and sea invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> around 1177 BCE, preserving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> while almost every other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> power collapsed or contracted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="Amarna letters" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Amarna_letters_on_display_at_the_British_Museum,_LondonA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Kingdom was administered through a centralised pharaonic system in which the king functioned simultaneously as divine ruler, supreme judge, and commander-in-chief. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>, one for Upper and one for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, managed day-to-day administration, overseeing taxation, judicial proceedings, and the distribution of state resources. Provincial governors administered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, the King's Son of Kush, exercised authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> as a semi-autonomous imperial sub-administration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna Letters</a>, the diplomatic archive recovered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s capital, reveal a sophisticated system of international correspondence conducted in Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> managed its relationships with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, and Alashiya (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>). Akhenaten's reign, from approximately 1353 to 1336 BCE, represented the most disruptive governance crisis in the empire's history: his elevation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> above all other gods, suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> cult, and relocation of the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> produced religious upheaval at home and diplomatic drift abroad, with Levantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> repeatedly requesting Egyptian military assistance against Hittite-backed rivals and receiving little response.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhetaten.jpg" alt="Akhetaten" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small_aten_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Markh, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Kingdom defined the cultural, diplomatic, and commercial architecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> diplomatic system, in which great powers exchanged elaborate gifts and correspondence to maintain equilibrium, represented the most sophisticated framework for inter-state relations the ancient world had yet produced. Egyptian artistic styles, religious concepts, and architectural forms radiated outward across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> through conquest, trade, and the deliberate Egyptianisation of foreign elites. Nubia under New Kingdom administration was transformed so thoroughly that the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite kingdom</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> adopted Egyptian gods, titles, and monumental forms, and would eventually conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> itself as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</a>. The disintegration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> trading network amid the upheavals of around 1200 BCE permanently undermined the New Kingdom's economic foundations, demonstrating how dependent the empire's prosperity was on the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> commercial system.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse.jpg" alt="Late Bronze Age collapse" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Age_End.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexikoua, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The New Kingdom's decline was structural rather than sudden, driven by the convergence of three processes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Bronze Age Collapse</a> from approximately 1200 BCE severed the trade networks that sustained imperial revenues and eliminated the Hittite rival whose existence had given Egyptian foreign policy its organising logic. Simultaneously, the progressive accumulation of land and institutional power by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> priesthood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> created a theocratic counter-authority that the weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> could not suppress. The assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harem-conspiracy">harem conspirators</a> around 1153 BCE, documented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-papyrus-of-turin">Judicial Papyrus of Turin</a> and supported by forensic examination of his mummy, inaugurated a succession of short-lived rulers bearing his name but little of his ability. By approximately 1080 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herihor">Herihor</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> as a de facto independent ruler, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smendes">Smendes</a> controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a> around 1070 BCE ended the New Kingdom not through conquest but through administrative dissolution.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Egyptian New Kingdom endures as one of history's most compelling demonstrations that imperial greatness rests as much on diplomacy and cultural magnificence as on military conquest.</em></p>

